1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,909
For the record, this is why I did it.

2
00:00:04,909 --> 00:00:12,000
A private Android journal for the reasons behind your work and wallet decisions.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,103
The app starts with something useful.

4
00:00:15,103 --> 00:00:21,828
Create a project and choose the one thing you want to move forward.

5
00:00:21,828 --> 00:00:27,000
Your first work session needs no account, wallet, or network.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,870
Here is the actual Android emulator recording.

7
00:00:31,870 --> 00:00:40,217
I create a project, write an intention, and choose a focus duration.

8
00:00:40,217 --> 00:00:45,087
The timer gives me room to work.

9
00:00:45,087 --> 00:00:49,261
I can pause or finish early.

10
00:00:49,261 --> 00:00:59,000
This demonstration shows an early finish after a few seconds.

11
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,609
At the end, I record what actually changed.

12
00:01:01,609 --> 00:01:03,891
A blocked attempt can be useful too.

13
00:01:03,891 --> 00:01:07,804
The next action is a small, specific place to start next time.

14
00:01:07,804 --> 00:01:11,717
The app saves progress only when I choose to save a record.

15
00:01:11,717 --> 00:01:14,000
A timer does not invent an outcome.

16
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,286
My last next action comes back on the home screen.

17
00:01:18,286 --> 00:01:21,714
The next visit has a clear starting point.

18
00:01:21,714 --> 00:01:26,000
Core work journaling continues offline without a wallet or account.

19
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,073
The project book makes previous work easy to revisit.

20
00:01:29,073 --> 00:01:34,195
I can see my starting intention, read the result, and continue from the next action.

21
00:01:34,195 --> 00:01:35,902
My own writing remains editable.

22
00:01:35,902 --> 00:01:40,000
This gives the next visit context without asking me to remember everything.

23
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:41,905
English is the default.

24
00:01:41,905 --> 00:01:47,619
Settings also offer Korean, Japanese, Simplified Chinese, Hindi, Spanish, Portuguese, and French.

25
00:01:47,619 --> 00:01:50,952
My own writing keeps its original language.

26
00:01:50,952 --> 00:01:54,286
The language choice belongs to the user.

27
00:01:54,286 --> 00:02:00,000
Fluent speakers should still review the translations before a broad public release.

28
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
The journal uses native Mobile Wallet Adapter to select an account and reads supported public Solana activity directly.

29
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:12,500
No Firebase billing upgrade or paid server is needed for this path.

30
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:16,667
Public RPC has rate limits and no production service guarantee.

31
00:02:16,667 --> 00:02:22,083
A successful real-wallet run on a physical device remains an open evidence gate.

32
00:02:22,083 --> 00:02:25,000
Optional cloud rooms and purchases remain separate.

33
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,091
Work records and reflection text stay on the device.

34
00:02:29,091 --> 00:02:32,273
JSON export gives me a portable copy.

35
00:02:32,273 --> 00:02:35,000
Reminders, haptics, and motion are optional.

36
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:39,545
The local workflow stays useful when the network is unavailable.

37
00:02:39,545 --> 00:02:45,000
The journal does not execute trades, forecast returns, or reward trading volume.

38
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:50,270
The next milestone is to prove the wallet-to-reflection path on a real device.

39
00:02:50,270 --> 00:02:53,108
The offline preview is ready to try.

40
00:02:53,108 --> 00:02:56,757
For the record, this is why I did it.

41
00:02:56,757 --> 00:03:00,000
Source and release materials accompany this submission preview.
